# =============================================================================
# AquariusOS — controllers Steam's own rules do not cover
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS IS, IN PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# A game controller plugged into Linux shows up as more than one thing at once.
# Two of them matter here:
#
#   /dev/input/event*   THE SIMPLE VIEW. "Button 3 went down, left stick moved
#                       to here." Every game and every desktop can read this,
#                       and Linux hands it to whoever is sitting at the screen
#                       automatically.
#
#   /dev/hidraw*        THE RAW VIEW. The actual conversation with the pad's
#                       own chip: which model are you, what is your serial
#                       number, set the light bar to this colour, rumble like
#                       that, here is the gyroscope. Linux does NOT hand this
#                       out automatically. By default it is root's alone.
#
# Steam drives every modern controller — PlayStation-class pads, and the many
# pads that pretend to be one — over the raw view. So a pad whose /dev/hidraw*
# file is root-only is a pad Steam can SEE and cannot USE. That is the exact
# shape of the fault: the controller appears in Steam's list, and nothing you
# press does anything. There is no error message anywhere, because from Linux's
# point of view nothing went wrong — Steam asked for something it is not
# allowed to have, and stopped asking.
#
# WHAT `TAG+="uaccess"` DOES. It tells systemd "this device belongs to whoever
# is logged in at this screen". systemd then adds that person to the file's
# permissions, and takes it away again when they log out. It is the same
# mechanism that gives you your webcam and your sound card, and it is what
# Valve's own rules use.
#
# WHY WE SHIP THIS AT ALL. The `steam-devices` package (installed by
# build_files/68-gaming.sh) carries Valve's list, and it is long — but it is a
# list of exact USB ids, and a pad that is not on it gets nothing. Valve's
# Razer entries stop at product id 0x100b. The community `game-devices-udev`
# rules do not have these either, and as of Fedora 44 no such package exists in
# Fedora or Terra. So this file is ours.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS WHERE A NEW CONTROLLER GOES. When a pad is seen by Steam but does
# nothing, look it up with `lsusb` (or `udevadm info /dev/hidraw5`), find the
# `1234:5678` pair, and add a stanza here in the same shape. Two lines per
# device — one for the cable or dongle, one for Bluetooth — and one comment
# line saying what the device is in words. Then add its ids to the read-back in
# build_files/68-gaming.sh, so a future edit cannot quietly lose it.
#
# FOUND ON THE BENCH, 2026-09-19. Royce's Razer Raiju V3 Pro was listed by
# Steam in Game Mode and did nothing at all. Its /dev/hidraw files were
# `crw------- root root` with no permission handed to him, while its
# /dev/input/event file was tagged `uaccess` and perfectly usable — which is
# why the pad looked present. Steam's own log says the rest: it read the pad
# through the simple view with a generic mapping, could not read its serial
# number ("Controller has an Invalid or missing unit serial number"), and then
# "Controller device closed after hid_read failure" at the exact second the
# pad changed mode and re-appeared with a different product id.
#
# THE PAD HAS TWO IDENTITIES, AND BOTH ARE HERE. The Raiju V3 Pro reports
# 1532:1026 in wired mode and 1532:1027 over its HyperSpeed wireless dongle —
# and it switches between them live, as the mode switch on the pad is moved.
# A rule for only one of them is a controller that works until it does not.
#
# Plain-language guide: docs/restart/gaming.md
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Razer Raiju V3 Pro
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wired mode (USB cable), and the HyperSpeed wireless dongle. `ACTION!="remove"`
# means "whenever this device appears or changes, but not as it goes away",
# which is the form every rule in Valve's own file uses.
ACTION!="remove", KERNEL=="hidraw*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1532", ATTRS{idProduct}=="1026", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
ACTION!="remove", KERNEL=="hidraw*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1532", ATTRS{idProduct}=="1027", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# The same pad over Bluetooth. A Bluetooth device has no USB vendor and product
# attributes to match on — the ids live in the name of the parent device
# instead, which is why this shape looks different for the same pad. Valve's
# rules do exactly this for their other Razer entries.
ACTION!="remove", KERNEL=="hidraw*", KERNELS=="*1532:1026*", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
ACTION!="remove", KERNEL=="hidraw*", KERNELS=="*1532:1027*", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
